* Agentic intrusion detection for Splunk: tempo, breadth, and concurrency.
*
* No indicators were published for the July 2026 Taiwan campaign, so these searches profile
* HOW the intruder behaved rather than what it touched.
*
* THE CORE IDEA
* -------------
* Three actors produce three different rhythms in an authentication log.
*
*   human    slow, irregular, long gaps. Seconds to minutes. Technique changes follow
*            reading and thinking, so they are rare and slow.
*   script   fast and uniform. Sub-second intervals, very low variance, and crucially it
*            does NOT change technique on failure. It continues or it stops.
*   agent    machine-speed BURSTS separated by INFERENCE LATENCY, typically 2 to 30 seconds,
*            and it CHANGES TECHNIQUE after a failure because a planner re-ranked its
*            options.
*
* That third pattern is not something the other two produce. It is a property of
* inference-driven planning, so it survives the operator swapping frameworks, models, or
* infrastructure. It is the most durable signal in this pack.
*
* TUNE BEFORE ENABLING. The thresholds below are reasoned from published inference
* latencies, not measured from the campaign, whose telemetry was never released. Baseline
* your own estate first. Every search here is written to be run interactively before it is
* ever considered as an alert.
*
* Author: Cristian Ruvalcaba and the Saluca Agentic AI Research Team, 2026-08-20
* Ref: https://securityaffairs.com/197079/apt/china-linked-hackers-use-ai-agents-in-autonomous-attack-on-taiwan.html
* License: Apache-2.0


* ===========================================================================
* 1. TEMPO PROFILE - the burst / think-pause / adapt signature
* ===========================================================================
* Scores each source on how agent-shaped its rhythm is. Run over 24h to start.
* Read the output as a ranking to investigate, not as a verdict.

index=auth OR index=wineventlog (action=success OR action=failure)
| fields _time src_ip user dest action signature EventCode
| eval technique = coalesce(signature, EventCode, action)
| sort 0 src_ip _time
| streamstats current=f window=1 last(_time) AS prev_time last(technique) AS prev_technique
    last(action) AS prev_action last(dest) AS prev_dest BY src_ip
| eval delta = _time - prev_time
| where isnotnull(delta) AND delta >= 0
*  Classify each interval into the three regimes.
| eval band = case(
      delta < 1.0,                 "burst",
      delta >= 2.0 AND delta <= 30.0, "think",
      delta > 60.0,                "human",
      true(),                      "other")
*  The adaptation signal: technique changed immediately after a failure.
| eval adapted_after_failure = if(prev_action="failure" AND technique != prev_technique, 1, 0)
*  A think-pause that precedes a technique change is the strongest single interval:
*  that is the shape of "the planner considered, then chose differently".
| eval planner_pause = if(band="think" AND technique != prev_technique, 1, 0)
| stats count AS events
        sum(eval(band="burst")) AS bursts
        sum(eval(band="think"))  AS thinks
        sum(eval(band="human"))  AS human_gaps
        sum(adapted_after_failure) AS adaptations
        sum(planner_pause) AS planner_pauses
        dc(dest) AS distinct_targets
        dc(user) AS distinct_users
        dc(technique) AS distinct_techniques
        stdev(delta) AS delta_stdev
        avg(delta) AS delta_avg
        min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen
    BY src_ip
| where events >= 20
| eval burst_ratio = round(bursts / events, 3)
| eval think_ratio = round(thinks / events, 3)
| eval human_ratio = round(human_gaps / events, 3)
| eval duration_min = round((last_seen - first_seen) / 60, 1)
*  SCORING. Agent-shaped means: meaningful bursts, a healthy think band, few human-length
*  gaps, and observed adaptation. Scripts score high on burst and near zero on think and
*  adaptation. Humans score high on human_ratio.
| eval score = round(
        (burst_ratio * 25)
      + (think_ratio * 30)
      + (if(adaptations > 0, 20, 0))
      + (if(planner_pauses >= 3, 15, 0))
      + (if(distinct_techniques >= 3, 10, 0))
      - (human_ratio * 30), 1)
| eval verdict = case(
      score >= 60, "AGENT-SHAPED - investigate",
      score >= 40, "possible agent - review",
      burst_ratio > 0.8 AND adaptations = 0, "likely script",
      human_ratio > 0.5, "likely human",
      true(), "unclear")
| table src_ip verdict score events duration_min burst_ratio think_ratio human_ratio
        adaptations planner_pauses distinct_targets distinct_users distinct_techniques
        delta_avg delta_stdev
| sort - score


* ===========================================================================
* 2. BREADTH WITH ADAPTATION
* ===========================================================================
* Scanners are broad and uniform. Humans are narrow and adaptive. Agents are broad AND
* adaptive: the Taiwan operator mapped 21 government systems in four days while changing
* technique between them. This search isolates that combination directly.

index=auth OR index=wineventlog
| fields _time src_ip user dest action signature EventCode
| eval technique = coalesce(signature, EventCode, action)
| bin _time span=1h
| stats dc(dest) AS targets_this_hour dc(technique) AS techniques_this_hour
        dc(user) AS users_this_hour count AS events BY src_ip _time
| stats sum(events) AS total_events
        max(targets_this_hour) AS peak_targets_hr
        max(techniques_this_hour) AS peak_techniques_hr
        dc(_time) AS active_hours
        avg(techniques_this_hour) AS avg_techniques_hr
    BY src_ip
*  Broad AND adaptive AND sustained. Any one of these alone is ordinary.
| where peak_targets_hr >= 5 AND avg_techniques_hr >= 2.0 AND active_hours >= 3
| eval note = "broad + adaptive + sustained: scanner-like reach with human-like variation"
| table src_ip note total_events peak_targets_hr peak_techniques_hr avg_techniques_hr active_hours
| sort - peak_targets_hr


* ===========================================================================
* 3. CONCURRENCY - several agents, different jobs, one origin
* ===========================================================================
* Up to eight agents ran at once, "each working a different angle". That is the distinction
* from a threaded scanner, which parallelises the SAME action. Here the parallel streams are
* doing DIFFERENT things at the same moment, which is what a coordinated team looks like.

index=auth OR index=wineventlog
| fields _time src_ip user dest action signature EventCode
| eval technique = coalesce(signature, EventCode, action)
| bin _time span=1m
| stats dc(technique) AS concurrent_techniques dc(dest) AS concurrent_targets
        count AS events values(technique) AS techniques BY src_ip _time
*  Four or more genuinely distinct activities inside one minute from one source.
| where concurrent_techniques >= 4 AND concurrent_targets >= 3
| eval note = "multiple distinct activities in parallel from one origin"
| table _time src_ip note concurrent_techniques concurrent_targets events techniques
| sort - concurrent_techniques


* ===========================================================================
* 4. CORRELATION - inference traffic beside authentication activity
* ===========================================================================
* The highest-confidence search in this file, and the cheapest to justify to a stakeholder.
* If a host is calling an inference API in the same minutes it is generating authentication
* activity, an LLM is in the decision loop. Nothing else explains the pairing.
*
* Requires proxy or DNS telemetry joined to auth. Defeated by self-hosted inference.

index=proxy OR index=dns
| eval inference = if(match(coalesce(url, query, dest_host),
      "api\.anthropic\.com|api\.openai\.com|openrouter\.ai|generativelanguage\.googleapis\.com|api\.mistral\.ai|api\.groq\.com|api\.deepseek\.com"), 1, 0)
| where inference = 1
| bin _time span=1m
| stats count AS inference_calls BY src_ip _time
| join type=inner src_ip _time [
    search index=auth OR index=wineventlog (action=success OR action=failure)
    | bin _time span=1m
    | stats count AS auth_events dc(dest) AS auth_targets BY src_ip _time ]
| where inference_calls >= 3 AND auth_events >= 3
| eval note = "inference API traffic interleaved with authentication activity from one host"
| stats sum(inference_calls) AS inference_calls sum(auth_events) AS auth_events
        max(auth_targets) AS peak_targets dc(_time) AS minutes_correlated BY src_ip
| where minutes_correlated >= 3
| table src_ip note inference_calls auth_events peak_targets minutes_correlated
| sort - minutes_correlated


* ===========================================================================
* 5. BASELINE HELPER - run this FIRST
* ===========================================================================
* Establishes what normal looks like in your estate before any of the above is trusted.
* Run over 30 days of known-good activity and use the percentiles to replace the thresholds
* in searches 1 to 3. Skipping this step is how a detection pack becomes a false-positive
* generator.

index=auth OR index=wineventlog (action=success OR action=failure) earliest=-30d
| fields _time src_ip dest action signature EventCode
| eval technique = coalesce(signature, EventCode, action)
| sort 0 src_ip _time
| streamstats current=f window=1 last(_time) AS prev_time BY src_ip
| eval delta = _time - prev_time
| where isnotnull(delta) AND delta >= 0 AND delta < 3600
| stats count AS intervals
        perc50(delta) AS p50_delta perc90(delta) AS p90_delta perc99(delta) AS p99_delta
        sum(eval(delta < 1.0)) AS sub_second
        sum(eval(delta >= 2.0 AND delta <= 30.0)) AS think_band
    BY src_ip
| eval burst_ratio = round(sub_second / intervals, 3)
| eval think_ratio = round(think_band / intervals, 3)
| stats perc50(burst_ratio) AS median_burst_ratio perc95(burst_ratio) AS p95_burst_ratio
        perc50(think_ratio) AS median_think_ratio perc95(think_ratio) AS p95_think_ratio
        perc50(p50_delta) AS typical_interval count AS sources
| eval guidance = "Set the burst and think thresholds in search 1 above your p95 values, not at the defaults"
| table sources typical_interval median_burst_ratio p95_burst_ratio median_think_ratio p95_think_ratio guidance
